  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(201.74pt, 447.36pt, "These findings provide unprecedented opportunities for advancing the ")
  #line-at(72pt, 468pt, "understanding of the orbital angular momentum dynamics in solids.   ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#242021"))
  #line-at(72pt, 507.84pt, "2. Results and Discussions ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(72pt, 549.12pt, "       For the light metal Ti, there have been relevant reports indicating that the orbital Hall effect ")
  #line-at(72pt, 569.76pt, "strengthens with the increasing thickness of Ti. We prepared samples ranging from 5 nanometers ")
  #line-at(72pt, 590.64pt, "to 60 nanometers. The Ti film were grown in Si (111) substrates by sputtering, as described in the ")
  #line-at(72pt, 611.28pt, "Supporting Information. Figure 2 presents the structural properties of Ti film. Figure 2. (a) gives ")
  #line-at(72pt, 631.92pt, "a wide-angle X-ray diffraction (XRD) spectrum for a 40-nanometer thick Ti film. Figure 2. (b) ")
  #line-at(72pt, 652.56pt, "shows XRD peaks for Ti films with different thicknesses. The dashed vertical lines in Figure 2. (b) ")
  #line-at(72pt, 673.44pt, "indicate the expected positions for the Ti (002) and Ti (101) peaks, and the dotted indicate the Si ")
  #line-at(72pt, 694.08pt, "(111) and Si (222) peaks, respectively. Figure 2. (c)  shows an atomic force microscopy (AFM) ")
]

